# ctxloom private state — the granular rules that REPLACE the old blanket
# `.ctxloom/*`. The blanket predates version-controlled content living under
# .ctxloom/content/, and left in place it silently un-tracks the project's own
# authored bundles: `git add` reports nothing and a content repo publishes an
# empty tree. These patterns are gitignore.PrivateStatePatterns; keep them in
# sync with that list rather than re-broadening to a blanket.
# The private-state rules now live in .ctxloom/.gitignore, which ctxloom owns
# and rewrites wholesale. Nothing about .ctxloom belongs here any more — an
# appended rule in this file is what accumulated five stale headers and
# attached them to a credential and three engine surfaces.
#
# This works because .ctxloom/ itself is NOT ignored. Were it ever blanket-
# ignored, git would not descend into it and the nested file would never be
# read — gitignore.Ensure already detects and retires such a blanket rule
# (SupersededBlanketLines / isSupersededBlanket), warning that it was hiding
# .ctxloom/content/ from git.
# Defense-in-depth: a NESTED .ctxloom under a package dir is test/tool pollution
# (the root `.ctxloom/*` above is anchored by its slash, so nested dirs slip
# through). The operations TestMain also prevents + guards this at its source.
internal/**/.ctxloom/
.mcp.json
.tasks/

# taskloom's OWN dot-dir (.taskloom/) is a DIFFERENT animal from .ctxloom/*
# above: config.yaml and (in repo-homed mode) tasks.jsonl are meant to be
# COMMITTED — that's the whole point of repo-homed homing (see
# internal/taskloom/config). Do NOT copy the .ctxloom/* blanket-ignore
# pattern here. Only the cross-process advisory filelock sidecar
# (internal/shared/filelock, taken at internal/shared/tasks/log.go) is
# private, ephemeral working state — ignore just that.
.taskloom/*.lock

