resources/schema/gen/

# Codex runtime state. ctxloom points CODEX_HOME at <project>/.codex to keep
# prompts cell-local (internal/codex/surfaces.go), but codex uses ONE home for
# both its config surface AND all of its runtime state — so it also writes its
# session store, conversation memories, logs, goals, temp files, AND ITS OWN
# bundled system skills (.codex/skills/.system/*) here. config.toml is now
# EXCLUDED from the tracked exceptions below (white-dawn §2.2B): codex itself
# appends a machine-specific `[projects."<abs-path>"] trust_level` entry into
# it on first run, so a file codex mutates every run should not be tracked —
# matches internal/gitignore/gitignore.go's TransientArtifactPatterns, which
# already ignores .codex/config.toml for every OTHER project's generated
# .gitignore; this repo's own hand-authored block had drifted from that
# policy (it used to un-ignore config.toml) and a `[projects...]` entry for
# this very repo had already leaked into git history before this fix — see
# the task's final report. skills/ stays fully ignored too (unlike prompts/,
# it is NOT ctxloom-exclusive — codex ships its own .system/ skills there, so
# there is no clean "ctxloom-owned" subset to un-ignore). Only the
# ctxloom-generated prompts (never codex-mutated) stay tracked; everything
# else is engine state that must never be committed.
.codex/*
!.codex/prompts/

# Agent surface materialized by ctxloom; regenerated, never authored here.
.agents/

# Generated living-docs journey pages (like generated protobuf/JSON Schema):
# produced by `just gen-living-docs` from a passing acceptance run's capture,
# so they cannot be stale by construction. The docs CI workflow generates
# them fresh before `npm run build`; see .github/workflows/docs.yml.
website/src/content/docs/journeys/

# opencode engine surface (generated when the opencode backend materializes; do not track)
.opencode/
opencode.json
AGENTS.md
.ctxloom-opencode-managed

# codex engine surface materialized by ctxloom; regenerated, never authored here.
.codex/config.toml

# Serena's per-project state. Only the LOCAL and rebuildable parts are ignored;
# .serena/project.yml and .serena/memories/ are VERSIONED deliberately.
#
# Serena ships its own .serena/.gitignore excluding exactly the machine-local
# parts (/cache, /project.local.yml), which is the tool telling us the rest is
# meant to be committed. Versioning it is also what makes settings and memories
# available across git worktrees, where each worktree activates itself.
#
# Ignoring the whole directory cost us once already: project.yml drifted against
# a schema key rename, serena died at startup with a KeyError, and its symbol
# tools were silently absent from sessions rather than failing loudly. An
# unversioned config cannot drift visibly.
.serena/cache/
.serena/project.local.yml

# Working decisions log — for human review during a work session, not part of
# the repo record. FINDINGS.md and docs/behaviour-changes-*.md are the committed
# artifacts; this is the scratch ledger behind them.
DECISIONS.md

# codex credentials — a real secret, nothing to do with ctxloom state.
.codex/auth.json

# Coverage-instrumented ctxloom (just build-cover). Same name as the shipped
# binary on purpose — see cmd/ctxloom/justfile — so it needs its own directory.
.coverbin/

# ctxloom private working state (rebuildable/local — cache, sessions, project id, local state)
.ctxloom/cache/
.ctxloom/sessions/
.ctxloom/project-id
.ctxloom/state/
.ctxloom/*.lock
